{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Chess strategies operate in a closed system with defined rules, whereas scientific theories face an open, potentially infinite universe of facts.","Evolutionary selection has no conscious agent making a 'preference,' while Popper's scientist must actively choose which theory to act upon."],"overturn_conditions":["If it is demonstrated that surviving past tests in an open system logically increases the probability of surviving future tests, the analogy to non-probabilistic selection fails.","If the 'choice' in chess is shown to rely on implicit probabilistic assessments of the opponent's behavior, the disanalogy collapses."],"shared_structure":["Selection of an option based on past survival against rigorous challenges rather than predicted future utility.","Acceptance that survival does not guarantee future success but represents the best available heuristic for continued testing.","Distinction between the logical status of the survivor and the pragmatic outcome of choosing it."],"source_memory_refs":["mem_chess_strategy_01","mem_evolutionary_selection_03"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a chess player chooses a line that has survived grandmaster analysis not because it guarantees a win but because it is the hardest to refute, a scientist chooses a corroborated theory to maximize the chance of finding new errors.","The 'cost' in both domains is the inability to predict the opponent's (nature's) next move with certainty."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without smuggling in induction if reinterpreted as a 'critical preference' grounded in the logical asymmetry of falsification rather than probabilistic prediction; however, this defense costs the agent any rational assurance that the preferred theory will succeed in future applications, reducing scientific action to a non-justified leap of faith consistent with his fallibilism.","counte